  "slug": "plasma-membrane-er-52a1",
  "summary": "Fresh reports link mitotic phosphorylation to ER-PM contact loss and cortical remodeling, alongside kinase-phosphatase and cytoskeletal control of division and secretion.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is phosphorylation-controlled reorganization of cellular architecture during division. In one preprint, mitosis-specific phosphorylation of the ER-plasma membrane tether E-Syt2 drives loss of ERPM contact sites, and retaining them delays cell progression. In fission yeast, Pdk1 phosphorylates the eisosome component Pil1 to disassemble eisosomes and remodel plasma membrane and cortical ER at the division site for Mid1 recruitment. A structural preprint on the CPC-PP2A(B56) hub, mediated by Borealin motifs, extends the kinase-phosphatase theme to chromosome segregation fidelity. The MTCL1/2 study is less directly mitotic, but shows katanin and CAPZ recruitment coordinating Golgi-region microtubules and actin to support secretory vesicle docking. New here is the mechanistic resolution of phosphoregulatory nodes at membrane contacts, cortex, and segregation machinery.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-26"
}
